sam: Heads-up: found a parity quirk between our Java and Kotlin SDK samples that trips retries/timeouts on Android 13-14 emulators. Repro: 1) Start long streaming generation (chunked body), 2) Trigger auth refresh from UI, 3) observe stalled client + extra outgoing POSTs and eventual deadline.

ivy: interesting. Which SDKs/clients?

sam: Kotlin SDK sample uses `suspend` wrapper + OkHttpCall.awaitResponse(), Java sample keeps synchronous enqueue. Kotlin sample wires coroutine cancellation to call.cancel() in a finally block. Versions: Retrofit 2.9, OkHttp 4.10.0, Kotlinx-coroutines 1.7.1.

diego: could be call.clone / dispatcher interaction. If coroutine reschedules, the same OkHttp Call can be re-enqueued while dispatcher still has maxRequests/host cap reached -> requests stall, then when a connection recovers OkHttp retries without updated Authorization header.

sam: exact. Backend logs show second POST arriving without Authorization and checksum identical. Not replayable body but identical chunks.

imy-note: (typo) sorry.

ivy: two quick angles: 1) SDK parity: ensure Java and Kotlin samples use the same Call.Factory + single-threaded Dispatcher config so behavior matches; 2) avoid cancelling by calling call.cancel() from a different thread without joining — prefer cooperative cancellation via Call.await on same dispatcher.

maria: also try bumping OkHttp to 4.11.x. There's a header-propagation fix on retries.

sam: tried 4.11.1 in a branch and it reduced naked retries but didn't eliminate them on Pixel 8 emulator image.

diego: other mitigation: increase OkHttp Dispatcher.maxRequests and maxRequestsPerHost for sample app during heavy streaming tests so clone/reschedule races are less likely. For streaming endpoints, set retryOnConnectionFailure(false) to avoid silent resends of non-repeatable bodies.

ivy: about parity, we discovered our Java sample used a custom Call.Factory that rewraps Request to add headers at enqueue time, while Kotlin sample applied headers in an Interceptor. When retries happen at connection layer, interceptor headers can be lost in some edge cases while factory-wrapping survives. That's why behavior differed.

sam: aha. So fix is to standardize to Interceptor-based header injection + Authenticator for 401 flows and a single in-flight refresh singleton. But note: interceptors + non-repeatable bodies need careful handling if OkHttp retries.

maria: recommended sample-app changes: 1) central Authenticator with in-flight refresh dedupe (CompletableDeferred), 2) mark refresh requests with `X-Refresh: true` and skip auth logic on them, 3) streaming-client: `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)` + tuned Dispatcher, 4) upgrade to OkHttp 4.11.1+. Add note in devex README explaining why Call.Factory vs Interceptor matters.

diego: small snippet for single in-flight refresh (Kotlin): ```kotlin
private val refreshLock = Mutex()
private var refreshDeferred: CompletableDeferred<String>? = null
suspend fun refreshToken(): String = coroutineScope {
  refreshDeferred?.let { return@coroutineScope it.await() }
  refreshLock.withLock {
    refreshDeferred?.let { return@withLock it.await() }
    val def = CompletableDeferred<String>()
    refreshDeferred = def
    try {
      val token = networkRefresh() // suspend network call
      def.complete(token)
      token
    } catch (e: Exception) {
      def.completeExceptionally(e)
      throw e
    } finally {
      refreshDeferred = null
    }
  }
}
```

sam: thanks, will try that. Also going to standardize header injection across samples so parity tests pass.

